#!/usr/bin/env node
/** EH001 siman 013 full redo part 5: beit-meir, beur-hagra, turei-zahav. */
import { patchFile } from "./_patch-siman-utils.mjs";

patchFile("siman_013/beit-meir/part-001.txt", "beit-meir", {
  "1#_": `Hagahah: only that he may not cohabit with her in the house; and Beit Yosef is stringent with an oath; and it seems to my humble opinion that what seemed to them to be stringent regarding this entering her house more than other unmarried women suffices with the prohibition of yichud — meaning they hold a betrothed woman is like "they examined each other" even though we do not consider an arusah presumptively as "they examined each other"; perhaps they were concerned: if he enters her house regularly "they examined each other" will be done; and since betrothal and marriage are forbidden to him — it seemed to them to compare to a kohen who divorced from betrothal who is forbidden even to dwell with her in an alley (and see seif 10 in the hagahah and siman 11 seif 7, and Perach ch. 119; and it escaped him the Rosh in responsum klal 32 law 13 which is seif 7 of siman 11) — so too one should be stringent regarding this entering at least so "they examined each other" not be done; but if so there is a question for us, for unmarried women do not immerse and Torah study is forbidden; and this too is forbidden regarding a betrothed woman to enter her house (and perhaps it is different because marriage is permitted); and perhaps therefore Rama omitted the oath, for he holds Beit Yosef was not really stringent with an oath except in places where unmarried women immerse — not so for us who are sworn and stand from the prohibition of niddah on the majority, as is in Beit Yosef Yoreh De'ah siman 183: it seems to him that a mere warning suffices; if so plainly the stringency of the oath does not seem to him, for we do not find the like; at any rate in my humble opinion the law is with Chelkat Mechokek that the same applies to pregnant and nursing women, for since marriage is forbidden to him per their words — entering is stringent in this; and not like responsum of the Gaon siman 19 who wondered much at Beit Yosef; and I do not know why he did not wonder at the law in seif 10 in the hagahah that Rivash forbade one who married another's wet-nurse and divorced her to dwell with her in an alley — let him challenge his own words, for regarding prostitution there is no concern to curdle her milk from turning over and prostituting, and all Rivash's reasons he brought — and what stringency is this over other unmarried women; or that Rivash learned to be stringent from a kohen who divorced, whose prohibition is also not in relations without kiddushin as explained in Rambam ch. 7 of Hilchot Issurei Bi'ah, and Chelkat Mechokek agreed in this detail; and Hagahot Maimoniot; and see siman 119 s.k. 15 in Beit Shmuel; and nevertheless since betrothal and marriage are forbidden they were stringent the same regarding a wet-nurse whom the Sages forbade betrothal and marriage — for all that the Sages enacted they enacted as at first; and from this Beit Yosef learned to be stringent regarding a betrothed woman; and his wonder is nothing in my humble opinion; and in the end he raised Beit Yosef's words that in my humble opinion; and in particular what he wrote: and all this applies to a betrothed woman to whom he said as she abandoned herself to him she also prostituted with others because she is designated to him; and this view was never heard in siman 4 except regarding arus and arusah or one designated in his house for prostitution — and that is a dispute; but betrothed man and betrothed woman who do not fear him from the Torah — and from this Chelkat Mechokek raised wonder; and in my humble opinion there is no question on him; and from his words it is implied in Beit Yosef from what he cited his words at the beginning of the siman — and if his intent was to distinguish between prohibition and prohibition he would have been silent; and what Be'er Heitev brings responsum of Shevut Yaakov regarding this stringency from siman 119 where he wrote there: but permitted to enter her house even though she married since he does not dwell there and is not secluded with her — in my humble opinion he did not permit entering except incidentally to carry and give with another who dwells with her or with her husband; and even with her husband — not so here: if entering is not forbidden to him he will regularly go to her house and be alone with her properly — they saw fit to forbid; however the oath — since Rama omitted it — one should not be stringent; and so Shevut Yaakov raised.`,
  "2#_": `Pilegesh — see Chelkat Mechokek; for this entire statement I do not understand what he is dealing with and what he wants with this; and what he wrote "and in my humble opinion" — from where did he learn to distinguish her from Shulchan Aruch seif 3; and examine.`,
  "3#_": `One who betroths within ninety days — we place him under nidui — so Beit Yosef decided to rely here on the Rambam, not like Perush Rashi per responsum of the Ran; and there is a question: behold the Ran did not decide so except regarding pregnant and nursing, and in this many agree as is also in Tosafot; and somewhat implied likewise the Raavad's view since he did not challenge ch. 28; and this is the view of Mahari of Orleans; but within ninety days — from where that the Ran explains like Maharam against all disputants except Chelkat Mechokek; and one may say; and see Maggid Mishneh who challenged the Ran's words against each other even regarding pregnant; and examine. And behold after Beit Shmuel wrote in this law and seif 11 several matters that in my humble opinion I am compelled to elaborate somewhat: in the Gemara in this law we place him under nidui; and if he fled — fleeing helps; and in the law of seif 11 in the Gemara: if he married he removes with a get; and Rashi explained nidui is to divorce; and if he fled — therefore fleeing helps so as not to coerce him to divorce because he revealed his intent he does not wish to marry; and all commentators learned from him that if he married fleeing does not help and he is obligated to divorce specifically; and so in Rivash siman 396 and the Rambam who is the language of Shulchan Aruch explained Chelkat Mechokek who holds his law is not to divorce but nidui for transgressing the rabbinic decree; and if he fled exile atones and he is exempt; and Rivash adds he forced his explanation that if this law were to coerce removal they would not have said "if he fled fleeing helps"; we hear from this: if he married a pregnant woman — for all he is obligated to divorce — fleeing does not help for the Rambam to save him from coercion to divorce; and in this contradiction to Beit Shmuel s.k. 109 in his name; and in this law of seif 10 the Rambam further wrote: if he married within three months we separate them — and teaches us a novelty that this prohibition is astounding; and even if he married he need not give a get — and so in Rivash, not like Beit Shmuel s.k. 15 who wrote: comes to teach us not to say that since he already married we do not separate him; for from the Torah and because he had relations once the waiting was partially confused — these became pregnant he will eat more; and perhaps not yet pregnant; and see responsum Rosh klal 53 siman 7; but many disagree and hold if he married within three months he is obligated to remove with a get; and perhaps specifically regarding betrothal he explains like the Rambam per responsum of the Ran; and regarding marriage he omitted because of the dispute that even per the Rambam nidui is not to coerce divorce — nevertheless one may say even regarding betrothal; but regarding marriage he is obligated to divorce — parallel to pregnant woman; and in the law of seif 11 the Gemara did not mention nidui nor fleeing — only all commentators of the Rambam learned from explaining nidui; but to suffer on his own we hear that even if he married within three months even though he wrote only "we separate" — nevertheless he is liable for nidui for the bad act; and if so we also hear that even if he married a pregnant woman even if obligated to divorce — he is also liable for nidui or fleeing; except Beit Shmuel s.k. 33 wrote from Rivash's words it is implied even for the Rambam if he married and divorces we do not place under nidui since he nullified his act; and in my poverty I examined Rivash and I do not find a place with this implication except in what he answered because he was uncertain if there was only betrothal or also marriage to a wet-nurse; and the answer first: if he married — there is no one who says he need not remove with a get and we coerce with all coercions; and if he betrothed and did not marry — there is dispute for the Rambam we do not coerce; but it seems from his view we place under nidui; and it is implied to him from what he did not challenge the Rambam's "married" — even if he divorces he is liable for nidui; and he proved his reasoning; and this implication in my humble opinion is not certain — if this were his view to rule nidui's explanation like the Rambam he would truly have written so; but behold in the responsum if he betrothed he explains explicitly that in all he is obligated to divorce even regarding betrothal; and per Rashi's explanation it is obvious he need not write "or even if he removes with a get he is liable for nidui"; but for the Rambam he is always liable for nidui even when divorcing; and know that the Rambam — and he is Shulchan Aruch — "married and fled and afterward came and dwelt with his wife — it is of no consequence"; and why was he particular about fleeing — if you say as Beit Shmuel s.k. 28 wrote: the Rambam wrote "and if he fled he need not divorce"; and accordingly he also wrote s.k. 29: I already wrote for the Rambam the fleeing helps and he need not divorce — it seems he understood this fleeing works that he need not again remove with a get; and so is the plain implication of Chelkat Mechokek; but in my humble opinion it cannot be — for one question is very great: first per his explanation that fleeing stated regarding betrothal within three months was not stated to exempt from a get, for regarding betrothal in any case he needs a get; rather for atonement; from where does he learn regarding marriage that is explicit in the Gemara "he removes with a get" that fleeing saves from the get; and is it the Rambam's way to invent his law from his own mind; and further the essence of Beit Shmuel's implication stems from Rivash; and in Rivash it is explained he learned from the Rambam that he forced his explanation that regarding betrothal he need not give a get — otherwise fleeing would not help; and how could he hold that because of this he was particular here about fleeing to save from the get; and further that in this manner since the time already passed from the Torah for the Rambam who has no rabbinic fine at all — the essence of the get is only for child regulations as written nearby in the name of the Rashba that from taste alone in miscarriage even if he did not divorce she is permitted to him; and the same after nursing time; rather the clear matter is that the particularity about this fleeing is in what he concluded "it is of no consequence" — meaning he need not nidui because he was atoned by fleeing; and if he returned within the time he is certainly fit to be coerced for the get; and likewise if he fled and it was in bet din's power — nevertheless to coerce for the get within the time; possibly in the present that he fled and his place was unknown — and in this the matter was delayed until the time passed; and he wrote "it is of no consequence" that he was exempt from nidui; and all the more from the get since the time passed — and how will he divorce and tomorrow take her and it be rabbinic matters as mockery; and though I know Chelkat Mechokek's implication tends to Beit Shmuel's intent — nevertheless it seems Shulchan Aruch teaches my intent when he wrote seif 12: he transgressed and married — we place him under nidui unless he fled and he removes with a get — meaning the get mentioned in the Gemara; and nidui learned from the Rambam's explanation that it is for punishment a fortiori from betrothal unless he fled and is saved from nidui through exile; and nevertheless he removes with a get — and not like Beit Shmuel s.k. 29 who reversed Shulchan Aruch's intent; and per Chelkat Mechokek's explanation on the Rambam's language "married and fled," etc. — but our master holds in every case fleeing helps — this is certainly Beit Shmuel's intent; and all the above questions; and therefore it seems Shulchan Aruch was unwilling in the chapter of Chelkat Mechokek and his explanation as above.`,
  "4#_": `Further Beit Shmuel s.k. 29 wrote: and if he fled he need not divorce — so Tur and Rivash; in Rivash in my humble opinion it is explained to the contrary — for he concludes first on betrothing a wet-nurse that fleeing should not help to save from a get except regarding betrothal within three months; but regarding a wet-nurse even regarding betrothal fleeing does not help because of danger to the child; and again he wrote he does not wish to rely on his reasoning in this; and he wrote: however in all there is no difference since he did not flee and did not reveal his intent he does not wish to marry — we coerce him to remove and place under nidui until he divorces, per Rashi's view — explicitly his explanation even regarding betrothal if he did not reveal his intent that we coerce to divorce; and all the more regarding marriage — except like the Tur states; and Bach already wondered he contradicts himself: in the law within three months he did not mention that fleeing helps except regarding betrothal; and Rashi's reason is explained because he revealed his intent — from this we learn if he married fleeing does not help to save from a get; and how is it possible that here where the get is explicit in the Gemara it should be preferable that even for marriage he is exempt from the get through fleeing; and what Beit Shmuel wrote at the end "however in the Tur it is implied" — he wishes to interpret this — the mind cannot bear that though Tosafot distinguish thus — to the contrary the divorce in the Gemara is only for marriage; and if because per Rashi divorce is required even regarding betrothal within three months — possibly this prohibition is light; but the lenient view of fleeing that is found only regarding betrothal — to invent it for marriage to a pregnant woman because its prohibition is light — this was never heard; and also in the Rosh itself fleeing that helps for a wet-nurse was not mentioned except regarding betrothal; therefore in my humble opinion possibly the Tur abbreviated incidentally and his intent is only regarding betrothal; and thus I do not find explicitly in any commentary regarding marriage to a pregnant woman that fleeing helps to save from a get except Chelkat Mechokek above; and I already wrote what is difficult per the Rambam and per Rashi's explanation I do not find at all except in a responsum: and some commentators hold the same regarding marriage, for since he fled so as not to divorce — fleeing helps — and the same for wet-nurse and pregnant woman; its implication: even marriage to a pregnant woman suffices with fleeing to exempt from a get; and one may wonder: if so what is asked on 37a whether we are lenient regarding a kohen because if he divorces she is forbidden to him — and it should be taught that he has a remedy through fleeing; and perforce he holds fleeing saves only if he fled of his own accord; but we do not invent this advice for him and it is also not effective; and so in Darkhei Moshe in the name of Or Zarua; and I need to ask regarding a shoteh who has no mind to flee; and examine; and with this there is a question on Rama in seif 10 in the hagahah who decided regarding a kohen forbidden to return to be lenient like the Rambam that regarding betrothal within three months he need not give a get; and at the end he wrote we teach him to flee — and if he has this remedy from the Torah to be more lenient regarding her than an Israelite — behold from the place this leniency flows, i.e., regarding betrothal of a chalutzah — there is no other remedy; and it is a great strain to say that since the get is not incumbent on him because he is a kohen — automatically he is also exempt from fleeing; and examine.`,
  "5#_": `To the hagahah: and specifically regarding intentional — this Or Zarua invented; Mahari Weil and Terumat HaDeshen brought it; and nowhere did he mention from where he learned to distinguish, since for the rabbis the reason for the get is not because of a fine but concern that separation is insufficient and lest he have relations with her as explained in the Rosh; and in my humble opinion per what I examined in Terumat HaDeshen's words that Chelkat Mechokek abbreviated: and possibly because of this we are not lenient in the other matter of Or Zarua if he married unintentionally to permit him with separation — and it refers to what he brought before that they do not practice like Or Zarua in what he explained: he betrothed and did not marry a wet-nurse — he does not need a get — he wrote: and possibly, etc., as above; and there is a question: and because in one the law is not like him — so in the other; and it seems he holds Or Zarua all infer to distinguish between unintentional and intentional — from what follows the view of Mahari of Orleans who learns from sotah: where an additional prohibition was added that we did not see from him that he intended to transgress — parallel to marrying a wet-nurse, kinuy, and setirah — he does not need a get; so too betrothal without marriage even though we saw he intended to marry her and he is suspected of this prohibition — nevertheless when marriage was prevented from him it suffices and he need not give a get; and we do not suspect him of the prohibition of relations without blessing; and from this he learned the same even if he married unintentionally — separation suffices and he need not give a get to fence; for even regarding this prohibition he was not made suspected regarding intentional; and if so Terumat HaDeshen properly wrote: and possibly since in this we do not practice like him per She'iltot — so here; and with this it is resolved for me what Maggid Mishneh challenged on Terumat HaDeshen: and he wrote it was hidden from him that the Rosh disputes Or Zarua since he strained to reject proofs of Mahari of Orleans from the chapter even though from arus and arusah Abaye made in his rulings and it was complete unintentional — and per the above it is satisfactory: since our laws these depended on each other — there is a question on the Rosh on Or Zarua: the Rosh since he disputes R' Yosi — plainly he does not distinguish between unintentional and intentional; not so Or Zarua who distinguishes between betrothal and marriage — all the more there is room to distinguish between unintentional and intentional. After I wrote as above an incident came before me responsum of the Gaon — and I found in it good pearls in the name of the Gaon Rabbi Meir son of Rabbi Neriah av beit din and rabbi of the community Frankfurt siman 25 who wrote it was hidden from the later authorities the words of Maharam in responsum Maimon siman 25 from the incident where a kohen married a wet-nurse, etc., that it was complete unintentional per the rulings of R' Yosi; and nevertheless Maharam concludes she needs a get (and it is clear per his words that what was done per a Torah scholar's ruling is considered complete unintentional as Maggid Mishneh also seized in the absolute — and not like Chelkat Mechokek s.k. 8 who equates unintentional per a Torah scholar's ruling with Terumat HaDeshen's unintentional where he erred in reasoning, for Terumat HaDeshen speaks only of a scholar who teaches himself per his reasoning; but per a Torah scholar's ruling there is no greater unintentional as proven in rulings and beginning of Or Zarua); and the Gaon author as a question arose for him to equate the dispute between Maharam the Talmud and R' Yosi Or Zarua his teacher — strained to equate them; and in my humble opinion it cannot be and certainly he disputes; for what he wrote that what is said "and per R' Yosi they did so" — we can explain the return was per the majority after the marriage; only giving to the wet-nurse was before the marriage; and the body of giving and marriage they did without ruling; and after the marriage they informed R' Yosi and he ruled they should vow, etc.; and indeed this is somewhat strained; so I say: in my humble opinion even with strain it cannot be brought into the intent of the statement; and also it cannot be, for per R' Yosi's view there is remedy through return — there is a question on the rabbis who hold Yevamot 37b: if she married she removes specifically with a get; until we coerce to remove we did not give to the wet-nurse with return; and even if you strain it deals with one who does not remove thus — still there is a question: and what is asked regarding a kohen if they made him a remedy; and so here there is remedy with a wet-nurse; and shall we say because it is uncommon to invent a wet-nurse thus they make remedy for a kohen; rather in my humble opinion R' Yosi follows the Raavad's view that I brought seif 12: even the rabbis' reasons are because of a fine if he married in prohibition and did not give to the wet-nurse — not because of child regulations.`,
  "6#_": `And the truth: if it were not said the beraita that teaches: if he married he removes with a get — implying there is no further remedy — accordingly one would also say like the Raavad that the reason is because of a fine; but per the many other commentators perforce he disputes and halakhah is not as he wrote; but for R' Yosi perforce even per halakhah it is proven he follows the Raavad's view; and if so it cannot be the incident was without a Torah scholar's ruling and in prohibition — for then how did he rule remedy after he committed a prohibition; or everything was done per his ruling. Further he wrote a second answer for R' Yosi: he did not know the wet-nurse has a husband, for certainly he would not err in this matter, for whoever has a husband has no remedy for the infant lest she become pregnant and her milk curdle; and what teaches "she gave her son to a wet-nurse" — we can say specifically an unmarried woman; but for R' Yosi it was not known she has a husband and he permitted through an oath; and the kohen and woman of their own accord gave to the wet-nurse — it cannot be; and if so in this they erred on their own without Torah scholars' rulings; so he concludes; and also this is not one — it is compelled to conclude; and Maharam hung the reason for the prohibition that the husband can annul for her — Terumat HaDeshen that one can say there is concern lest she become pregnant; except he wrote for amplification that even if she does not become pregnant there is concern she will return through annulment; and this one may hang the prohibition on annulment; and this does not forbid per his view, for this ruling that we are not concerned for this per R' Yosi's rulings they did — and he does not hang the obligation to remove on the truth that it is because they acted of their own accord to give to the wet-nurse; and further he compares this error to Terumat HaDeshen's law if one acts per his own rulings and reasoning — and this is not so, for the teacher ruled generally to give to a wet-nurse through return — it turns out he told them a general rule to give to a wet-nurse and a particular that specifically through return; and what is included is only what is in the particular; and there is no greater coercion than this: from where would their minds arise that this is specifically an unmarried woman presumptively a wet-nurse — it cannot be; and further per his view that the wet-nurse in the Talmud is specifically unmarried (and below we will speak) — certainly the act in the hagahah seems the R' Yosi argued even to his disputants that return does not help; nevertheless because he is a kohen there is room post factum to rely on the beraita "she gave to a wet-nurse — permitted"; and Maharam disagreed — in his name his reason with strain; and if as his words behold the beraita deals specifically with unmarried; and on whom should one rely; or all this does not exist; again he wrote a third way to distinguish from Or Zarua's reasoning: he deals while her milk had not yet stopped; and Maharam deals when it stopped — and this is not in my humble opinion but prophetic words; and even an amora — and all the more poskim; and also the rabbis' reason in the get is explained in the Rashba because separation is not a remedy — we are concerned nevertheless he will have relations with her; even for one who wants to say their reasons are because of a fine — therefore it is clear like the Gaon Maharam son of Rabbi Neriah who ruled Maharam is not like Or Zarua; and I already brought below the view of all the rishonim not like him; and my intent should be understood: halakhah — one should not rely at all more than the teacher in folly — Shulchan Aruch; and perhaps Rama retracted in Shulchan Aruch from what he wrote in Darkhei Moshe.`,
  "7#_": `Hagahah: but if he betrothed unintentionally — see Terumat HaDeshen's words in Beit Shmuel; and Terumat HaDeshen wrote this from his own reasoning and also brings proof from what he decided the law of a kohen who betrothed a chalutzah without chalitzah in siman 164 seif 7 — deals only with unintentional; and in my humble opinion it does not seem so per the poskim there; and in particular per what is explained in Mahari Weil that man and woman must be unintentional; and in siman 164 it is implied she is intentional from halakhah and halakhah and was betrothed — which is the language of intentional.`,
  "8#_": `Hagahah: if he divorced her — forbidden to dwell, etc. (Rivash siman 396) — examine: behold in Rivash it is said only regarding marrying a pregnant woman; and he compares to a kohen who divorced even though this is Torah and this rabbinic — all that they enacted, etc.; and siman 119: even regarding a kohen who divorced from betrothal permitted to dwell with her in an alley if not "they examined each other"; and if so from where to be stringent here regarding plainly one who divorced from betrothal; and perhaps because he hurried to betroth in a significant prohibition it is considered to him as certainly brazen.`,
  "9#_": `Another's pregnant woman — see Beit Shmuel who wrote: even though rejected, etc. — nevertheless he did not retract, etc.; accordingly we also say per the reason of pregnant — dried breasts forbidden also per the second reason; and see ch. 20 ch. Af Al Pi — per his words it is not compelled, for he explains well Rabbenu Tam's proof that his inference is from the first reason of pregnant — even regarding a divorcee and the same nursing; and it was at least proven child regulations apply to nursing even though not obligated from the Torah for the month — for the conclusion "presumptively" is only regarding a widow; but in any case plainly possible for the Raavad pregnant refers also to a divorcee; and per the conclusion the reason changed the explanation and refers only to a widow; and if so one may say properly even though the Raavad deals also with dried breasts — per the conclusion not with dried breasts; however nevertheless the law is true that without proof it cannot be that pregnant with dried breasts is permitted; for regarding nursing they said dried breasts is not called nursing and she is not included in the decree; but pregnant plainly is called forbidden; and one may say properly this is the Rambam's reason; and he holds even though we conclude not from that reason — fitting to decree; nevertheless since they decreed from nursing they again did not distinguish and decreed also on dried breasts from the reason "lest she become pregnant"; and Chelkat Mechokek already wrote: possibly he did not read except; and in my humble opinion further: even at minimum those who do not mention the reason "lest she become pregnant" — nevertheless they agree as above; and they hold specifically nursing with dried breasts — "presumptively" is not decreed on account of presumptive nursing in general that has a voice as in the Rosh that certainly recognizable between she gave her son to a wet-nurse and did not give; and this does not apply to pregnant; and there is room to decree; and not like Beit Shmuel's implication that per the elder Rosh pregnant with dried breasts is permitted; and some are lenient regarding a zona — see in this responsum.`,
  "10#_": `And the month of pregnancy, etc. — see responsum of the Gaon siman 25; and "and some say if she swore," etc. — he wrote in responsum Kenesset Yechzekel siman 59; and my heart tells me that which Rabbenu Nissim and his colleagues permitted to give to an unmarried wet-nurse — but a married woman — what relevance; perhaps she will become pregnant, etc.; and I wondered he did not mention any explanation of this matter; and it is clear to me like the sun; and in responsum 52 he was particular about this in the first question of these matters siman 13.`,
  "11#_": `And the truth: I too, poor in understanding, always went with this thought until from it I received in a responsum a question on Shulchan Gavoah ch. HaCholeitz who permits a nursing divorcee even if he recognizes her to marry like a gentile woman; and I wrote that in my humble opinion even a gentile woman who nursed until recognition is forbidden to marry; and proof from Rabbenu Nissim who permitted in the house of Rabban Gamliel; and it is clear as Kenesset Yisrael wrote: for an unmarried woman they hired — and are the house of Rabban Gamliel robbers; and behold we hold in Chelkat Mechokek siman 353 seif 7: but if there are workers to hire, etc.; and all the more a worker who can return even in half a day; or that in this manner where there is another there is no concern except concern there will not be found another or she will retract after recognition — this country is not permitted to retract — there is no greater lost matter; but regarding others nevertheless there is concern within the time of coercion danger — not so in the house of Rabban Gamliel; and if it were that she is permitted to marry still even the house of Rabban Gamliel did not release that she nurse until recognition and again marry; or that even a gentile woman after recognition or without another being found she is delayed from marrying; so there; and however now I found in responsum of the Gaon siman 25 a challenge in the name of the Gaon Rabbi Meir son of Rabbi Neriah av beit din of Frankfurt on responsum of Maharam in responsum Maimon letter siman 25: what helps that she gave to a wet-nurse — behold she was married; and in the Gemara when they said "they gave to a wet-nurse" — meaning unmarried; and behold this challenge on the entire principle emerges against R' Yosi who permitted and all the disputants there; and in particular all Ashkenaz scholars were the disputants as explained there and in responsum of the Rosh; and there is none who would sense such a plain matter; and in particular Maharam who established the foundation of the prohibition because she has a husband; and there is room to permit the oath with strain — in his name; and also R' Yosi who returned and argued because he is a kohen there is room to rely on the beraita "she gave to a wet-nurse — permitted to marry"; and Maharam disagrees also with strain; and per the above there is no leniency from the beraita that deals specifically with unmarried; and see above; and I gave to my heart: behold he spoke only; and certainly the view of the rishonim from me is elevated until I found it should be understood: possibly the law is with them; for in any case uncertain what we learned regarding a divorcee: and if he recognizes her we coerce her and nurse for wages; and so in Shulchan Aruch siman 82; and there is a question: behold we conclude in the sugya the husband may have relations with his nursing wife — if she becomes pregnant he will feed him eggs — implies feeding eggs suffices even if he nursed until recognition; if so what is the necessity to coerce the divorcee — he will feed him eggs; and what does he care about child care; and truth one may say per taste Rambam ch. 1 of Hilchot Gerushin explained feeding eggs are medicines for the mother so her milk not curdle — and it fits; however for all our rabbis except him — certainly there is a question; and perforce they hold specifically a woman with her husband suffices for the child — feeding eggs even if he recognizes because she is in any case with her mother who recognizes and his longing for her; and immediately the feeding stops at the nursing place — not so a recognizing divorcee — how shall we act; the father will give egg-feeding wages to the mother — there is no remedy for the child for sometimes the money will end or be lost immediately or from the third parties — if he gives weekly there is concern a woman is ashamed to come to bet din and in particular with her divorcer; and plainly if he does not recognize even if there is no other found she can say what do I care for your child's care — take him and feed him eggs (and with this it seems in my humble opinion what is said siman 82 in the hagahah: but if he has no — meaning even money for feeding eggs; but if he has — what of it that he does not remove the wet-nurse and feed him eggs unless we are concerned per the Rambam's explanation); not so regarding recognition — presumptively an infant who recognizes upon separation from his wet-nurse has longing for her and there is danger; therefore we coerce her; and further one may infer in the beraita that permitted through a wet-nurse; and so Rabbenu Nissim who permitted in the house of Rabban Gamliel because there is no concern she will retract; and there is a question: perforce we must hold the explanation that a woman is ashamed to come to bet din; and though we are not concerned lest she perhaps retract — that is if she achieves what was fixed in her wages — nevertheless there is concern perhaps the one obligated for nursing wages will not give what was fixed for her; and a woman is ashamed to come to bet din and the breast will slip from her mouth and danger; or specifically if the infant is one she is ashamed to claim sustenance for her son as Rashi explained; but a gentile woman has no shame to claim from the obligator whoever he may be; and if so I do not find Rambam's explanation that feeding eggs are medicines so her milk not curdle — plainly regarding a gentile woman there is no concern if she has a husband that if she needs medicines she will claim from the obligator; but even per all commentators' explanation one may also say there is no concern that if her milk curdles and the child recognizes — this too is included since he recognizes and the infant's longing for her that she is obligated in any case to feed him eggs and eggs suffice for him as with a married woman; and shame — behold she has no claim; and with this the view of the rishonim rises, for certainly per their view is the implication of presumptive wet-nurse "she has a husband," for unmarried it is not common that she stands to nurse.`,
  "12#_": `And even Shulchan Gavoah's words in this — nevertheless correct: a gentile wet-nurse is permitted to marry even if he recognizes even though he himself ruled ch. Af Al Pi — his words were brought siman 82 in the hagahah that even gentile women who nursed until recognition we coerce them to nurse for wages — nevertheless properly she can marry since there is no shame for her to come to bet din; also no concern to curdle milk — then she will feed him eggs herself; but what he permitted from this even a divorcee even recognizing to marry — this is not in my humble opinion; and what was challenged to the Gaon author the aforementioned responsum "and further that a nursing widow even if she does not wish to marry should be forbidden to give her son to a married woman to nurse lest her milk curdle; and even for unmarried lest she retract and meanwhile her milk stops and the child dies"; and he wrote: and it should be said that if she does not marry even if she retracts or curdles her mother will feed him eggs or she will find another; and what he wrote "or she will find" is insufficient — perhaps she will retract after recognition; and per the above even the mother's feeding eggs remedy is insufficient after nursing from the wet-nurse until recognition and she is considered to him as mother and his longing for her; but in my humble opinion this is difficult from the outset: specifically if marriage was not forbidden to her there is concern she will give to a wet-nurse in order to marry; but when marriage is forbidden to her — presumptively mothers nurse their sons; and if she gives to a wet-nurse — mostly it is from necessity that she cannot nurse herself; and then certainly no concern she will retract — what could she do; and for the minority the Sages were not concerned; and if the wet-nurse is married — concern lest she become pregnant; per the above the opposite is satisfactory: even if it curdles after recognition the wet-nurse will feed them eggs and not the mother; and eggs suffice; and it is astounding.`,
  "13#_": `And he removes with a get even if he is a kohen — Beit Shmuel wrote; and if he married a pregnant woman and she miscarried — see siman 164; and meaning there s.k. 8; and in order to stand on the essence of the matters — for Beit Shmuel abbreviated — I will elaborate somewhat: Chiddushei Aggadot 37 regarding pregnant, etc., and he concludes that he too removes with a get even though she becomes forbidden to him automatically; nevertheless it is reasonable that regarding yevamah we do not coerce to remove so she not be forbidden to him forever; and one may say regarding pregnant to a kohen because she was bound to him and he erred in a mitzvah matter; and even though its time is not in haste — nevertheless since the essence of the matter for the rabbis does not exist from there child regulations and they did not fine to forbid her forever — here since he erred in Darkhei Moshe and if we remove her she will be forbidden forever we do not remove; or in general where there is only rabbinic prohibition — even though we explain it from it — if he does not remove with a get we are concerned lest he have relations with her; but here where there is Torah prohibition — if he informs him and explains he separates from her as we say above; and know that from the outset too she was bound and we were not concerned; or because there is mitzvah of yibum and chalitzah in place of yibum it is not a mitzvah for her; so his implication: he spoke on the yavam performing yibum with a clear pregnant woman that we do not remove her with a get while she is still pregnant as the second version precisely wrote; but here where there is Torah prohibition — i.e., during pregnancy — and it also seems he derived his law from the mishnah: "one who performs yibum with his yevamah and she is found pregnant — if the child is viable he upholds"; it is explained that until she gives birth we wait and separate; and as explained siman 164; and it was challenged to him: behold ab initio immediately when she is found pregnant he is obligated to remove with a get; and he wrote: and it is reasonable, etc.; and even if he performed yibum while she is pregnant — and it is very astounding; and what remedy is possible with a get or chalitzah while she is pregnant; plainly regarding one who marries a pregnant woman — his entry equates her to his wife — he is obligated to divorce to nullify his act and she be to him in the prohibition of an unmarried woman as before; but yibum with a pregnant woman — he accomplished nothing as we hold relations with a pregnant woman do not count as relations; and automatically even the get while she is pregnant accomplishes nothing; and all the prohibition he had before yibum and after yibum — this same prohibition itself will be after the get no more literally; and even for R' Yosi who holds relations with a pregnant woman count as relations — that is if she miscarries; but while she is pregnant what does the get accomplish; and even with chalitzah after the get she is still in zikah of six months upon him as before; and perforce it should be read he holds nevertheless since through this get she becomes disqualified to him even if she miscarries — this is for him a fence lest he not approach her since it is known that from then in every matter she is forbidden to him; and for this he answered with two answers; and in a third he wrote: or because there is mitzvah of yibum — meaning perhaps she will miscarry; and these are Beit Shmuel siman 164's words: or one may say he holds like Tosafot here because of mitzvah of yibum he should not divorce lest the child be a stillbirth, etc.; and Nekudot Yosef's plainness also seems he holds like the Rashba that it is fit to say while she is pregnant he removes with a get; and the Rashba's challenge was also raised from the mishnah and he distinguishes between marrying within three months and being found pregnant and marrying a pregnant woman — then immediately obligated to remove; so he wrote; and they challenged: in the mishnah when he marries his yevamah and she is found pregnant — why did the Sages not fine to remove with a get even though she becomes forbidden to him; and they answered: since her pregnancy was not recognized and he thought to do a mitzvah they did not wish to fine him; however if it was recognized they wrote: in this certainly we fine to remove through chalitzah even if the child is not viable; so he concludes; but if so I cannot reconcile Beit Shmuel's first answer on Tosafot in siman 164 after he proved per his view regarding yavam with pregnant woman and he did not divorce until after she miscarried — then there is no child regulation — he permits; even if he vowed, etc. — wishes to reject the Rashba's second answer; meaning from a kohen there is no remedy — implies even if he wishes to swear not to approach her — nevertheless obligated to divorce and she becomes forbidden; and the same for yavam; and the third answer also there if she miscarried she is permitted — meaning so Tosafot hold; and what of it — nevertheless there is a question: behold in any case even a kohen who marries a pregnant woman immediately obligated to divorce — then also even if she miscarries she is forbidden to him; and how is it possible in the mishnah "he upholds" — behold immediately he was obligated to divorce before she miscarried; and return his challenge on Tosafot to its place; however in my humble opinion Tosafot's precision is not compelled — one may say they agree with Nekudot Yosef only they stated in their language the first chapter in his name; and it is astounding; and the Raavad ch. 1 of Hilchot Gerushin after he challenged the Rambam per his view that betrothal within three months — we place under nidui to divorce — all the more marriage; and he added to be stringent: after three months she is forbidden to him as the time she was in prohibition; he wrote in any case; and mishnah Bava regarding his yevamah within three months: if the child is not viable — and it does not teach "he removes" there because she is a yevamah and if he gives a get she becomes forbidden to him from his language that he skipped; and she is found pregnant — and also it was not challenged to him except per his view that requires a get for one who marries within three months — not so for the Rambam who implies plainly that regarding pregnancy time from the outset there is no question why the mishnah teaches "and she is found pregnant" — how does separation suffice and not immediately a get like the law of recognized pregnant woman; this is a question even for the Rambam; and this is from the reason I wrote that I did not understand the Rashba's view; and so implied in Chelkat Mechokek who wrote and it was challenged to the Raavad; and why uphold since he is within three months; and so in Maggid Mishneh: therefore there is a question on the Raavad per his view, etc.; and why uphold if the child is not viable — and they did not say he divorces and returns after three months; and it seems they understood his challenge that the mishnah's implication even if she miscarried within three months too he upholds; and why behold nevertheless she is forbidden from the Sages' decree not to marry within three months even if she miscarried as explained seif 1; but if so their question and also Chelkat Mechokek's answer is uncertain; for Chelkat Mechokek answers on what the Raavad answered and challenged him from a kohen; and one may say he did not say so in the Gemara except regarding pregnant prohibition, etc.; but here since it was a light prohibition they made remedy; and we hear from this the same for a kohen regarding prohibition within three months they make remedy; and this is not like the Tur; and it is the first answer in the hagahah; and from the Torah the Tur should dispute him that the Raavad in what I do not find disputes him; and further what he wrote that it is a light prohibition — even though mentioned in Chelkat Mechokek per the Rambam — nevertheless Tosafot disagree as explained in Beit Shmuel in their name; and somewhat implied the Raavad holds like Tosafot, for in law 28 he does not challenge and it is implied he agrees regarding betrothal of pregnant woman he need not give a get even though regarding betrothal within three months he is stringent; and it is Mahari of Orleans's view; and further in yibum within three months where we add on waiting there is definite prohibition of brother's wife; and the challenge from the outset is not a challenge per their understanding — it deals with after she miscarried within three months, for after miscarriage the prohibition is only from R' Meir's decree and R' Yosi disputes Ketubot 60; and even though Tosafot wrote what they wrote in Yevamot 41b s.v. behold the minor — their words are not compelled except for R' Yosi and R' Elazar who we hold like them; there is no compulsion at all; and if so one may say regarding a kohen too who married within three months after she miscarried there is no obligation to forbid her since there is R' Yosi who permits and she is not comparable to pregnant or within three months before miscarriage — for per the Raavad all agree obligated to remove; and properly even a kohen he upholds; and one may say the commentators' challenge is on the time before miscarriage and before she is found pregnant — why they did not immediately require divorcing her like the law of kohen within three months; and on this they properly challenge: if so on the Rambam too there should be a question on the time "and she is found pregnant"; and further on this Chelkat Mechokek's answer does not go — behold during pregnancy the prohibition is severe; or that before miscarriage get is not relevant at all as above; or possibly the mishnah deals with the matter being delayed until she miscarried; and if so examine; however the truth: if the Raavad's challenge were as I understood from their words — per his view from the reason of decrees within three months — it was challenged to him to remove; and per what I strained that its implication from what is said "and she is found pregnant" and "miscarried" — all refers within three months as he explained — possibly to interpret their challenge as I wrote; but the Raavad's explanation is clear in Chiddushei Aggadot that he wrote after he brought the dispute of the Ramban with the Raavad regarding separating after ninety days she stood with him within ninety — and I say from the Raavad it is implied he holds we fine him (and see siman 26 seif 1); and support for his words: since we remove her with a get even so — implies it is a fine that requires fining; and if it were so that he stood with her ninety days — if you say he upholds — it turns out you do not fine him and "the wicked prolongs his wickedness and is rewarded"; and if you say he removes with a get and returns and marries immediately — you make rabbinic matters as mockery; and it does not exist, for that which requires for the rabbis a get even so — is not a fine except because of child regulations; and in place of child regulations they were not concerned for a kohen; and the rabbis never have a fine — for otherwise there is a question for me: behold regarding pregnant if he did not manage to divorce until she gave birth — then there is no further child regulation reason — it seems above he upholds from what Rava needs to establish R' Eliezer who said if the child is not viable he removes; and R' Meir on one view that for the rabbis he upholds since she already gave birth; and if it were that we fine even when she gave birth — all would say one matter that even the rabbis of R' Meir agree she removes even though she becomes forbidden forever automatically like pregnant to a kohen; and one may say: since the essence of the rabbis' reason is not to forbid her forever; and regarding yevamah there is because of mitzvah of yibum as I wrote — if it were R' Eliezer holds like the rabbis he would not remove her from him; but for R' Meir whose essence of reasons is because of a fine — and in general we forbid her forever — the same for yevamah — so per the Raavad's view. And like the Ramban it seems: behold the essence of the Raavad's inference to fine the rabbis is from the law of pregnant to a kohen; and he holds in all the mentioned matters — both within ninety days and pregnant and nursing — we always fine specifically to divorce at the time of permission as she was with him in prohibition; and even a kohen who married within ninety days and she delayed until afterward and is found not pregnant — nevertheless must divorce and she becomes forbidden forever; and the same for one who marries a pregnant woman and she miscarries or nurses and twenty-four months passed — nevertheless must divorce; and even a kohen except yibum because of mitzvah of yibum; and what the Raavad concludes there "because she is a yevamah," etc. — his reason is not because of his remedy and because of light prohibition as Chelkat Mechokek — rather so as not to nullify mitzvah of yibum we do not fine — not so a kohen who has no remedy even for the rabbis from fine decrees; and now what Beit Shmuel was uncertain regarding one who marries pregnant and miscarries whether he divorces — per the plain sense that he need not after we rule like all commentators and we do not require distancing at the time of permission as she was in prohibition — how is it possible he is obligated to divorce today and tomorrow take her and it be mockery; and all the more regarding a kohen who is permitted to him (and see below); and so married a wet-nurse and she delayed until afterward — he need not divorce today and tomorrow take her; and so explained in Shulchan Aruch: married and fled and afterward, etc. — it is of no consequence; and they were particular about fleeing — meaning per the Rambam to exempt nidui; and automatically for disputants even if he did not flee — it is of no consequence; and if indeed in siman 164 it is written in the hagahah: but if he married her after the fetus was recognized he must remove her even though she miscarried — it is implied even if the matter was delayed until she miscarried he is obligated to remove; and properly Beit Shmuel was challenged there: if even one who marries pregnant and miscarries is not obligated to divorce from the Torah — a yavam who is parallel to a kohen should be obligated; nevertheless the precise in Nekudot Yosef's language that I copied from which emerges Rama's hagahah — it seems per the Rashba's view that I copied from the beginning: the essence of his challenge was on pregnancy time whose implication as explained there in Shulchan Aruch is only we separate and do not remove with a get — on this it was challenged to him: what changed yavam from kohen who marries pregnant who divorces immediately because child regulations still apply to disqualify her to him — so the yavam should need to disqualify with a get; and on this he distinguishes between marrying before recognition and after recognition; and what he concludes "however if it was recognized they wrote," etc. — perforce must be strained from what he began the challenge that he removes with a get and concludes with chalitzah; and in any case if as Beit Shmuel understood and his plainness that even if delayed until miscarried he is obligated to remove — he should have said with a get and chalitzah as stated there seif 5; rather his intent seems: if he married a pregnant woman he must remove with a get during pregnancy time to disqualify her to him; and with chalitzah he must delay because while she is pregnant it accomplishes nothing; and then after she is disqualified to him already through the get when she miscarries — nevertheless he must remove her through chalitzah to permit her (though the language "and even though" is strained); but if the matter was delayed until she miscarried — one may say he agrees with the Rashba that from the Torah it is reduced from one who marries pregnant and miscarries since the reason passed she is permitted to uphold; and one may say as Beit Shmuel distinguishes and wrote: one may say specifically here where there is an opinion for yavam, etc.; for his essential inference is from pregnant to a kohen and suffices to come from the law, etc.; and one may also say forever that even if delayed until she miscarried he is obligated to divorce — as Rama's implication; and he truly holds like the Raavad who learns from pregnant to a kohen a fine for the rabbis; and he also holds all who marry in prohibition are obligated to divorce in days of permission as the days she was with him in prohibition even afterward; and automatically the same for kohen and yavam — this is explained in Nekudot Yosef itself not like the Raavad in his name; however there is another way to strain Nekudot Yosef's view: he learns in part from pregnant to a kohen that the rabbis also fined; and specifically where there is room to fine like a kohen where after the get she is forbidden forever; and also the law of justice was that he divorce while pregnant; therefore even after miscarriage or nursing after twenty-four months so the sinner not be rewarded for what he transgressed and did not divorce during prohibition time — he is nevertheless obligated to divorce afterward; and from this he learned the same for yavam who married after recognition — even after miscarriage he is obligated nevertheless to divorce and perform chalitzah; but it is clear in my humble opinion he agrees regarding a stranger who marries pregnant since for the rabbis the essence of their fines was never — automatically since he does not hold like the Raavad — there is no room to fine him; if he divorces today and tomorrow takes her it is mockery; and plainly for the time he need not divorce; and also he is not sinner rewarded — for even if he divorced during the present time it would be permitted to him; the entire Nekudot Yosef's view is uncertain, for this itself — kohen who delayed until she miscarried — from where that he is obligated to divorce; but this is clear: a stranger who married pregnant and miscarried — again he need not divorce; and examine; and from all the aforementioned rishonim one may learn they did not descend to distinguish between unintentional and intentional mentioned above in the name of Or Zarua; and even the Raavad who holds even for the rabbis divorce is because of a fine — since he strained to establish the mishnah's reason because of nullification of mitzvah of yibum and did not establish plainly that he is unintentional — all the more the Rashba per his view that for the rabbis divorce is only for child regulations or waiting; and even Nekudot Yosef who establishes the mishnah specifically without recognition and unintentional — behold he conditioned on him and thought to do a mitzvah — implies that in general unintentional we do not distinguish; and examine; again responsum of the Gaon came before me and I found he speaks on this matter — see there siman 18, for it seems to me he did not write so; and I again do not have time to deepen in this place.`,
  "14#_": `What we said — see Beit Yosef; and see responsum of the Gaon siman 14 who wrote: accordingly it is implied the elder Rosh permits even when he recognizes; and I was astounded exceedingly — behold Tosafot 60b: Rabbenu Nissim bar Yosef said: these are Beit Shammai's words, these are Beit Hillel's words; and per the elder Rosh's words where do we find it; and behold per Beit Shammai no woman is obligated to nurse, etc.; and it is strained: the essence of Beit Shammai's dispute was not said regarding marriage but regarding the essence of nursing time; and even though the infant nurses and goes until four and five years — nevertheless there is a difference if Beit Shammai explained: permitted to return him if explained before twenty-four months; and Beit Hillel forbids after eighteen months; for R' Meir and R' Yosi dispute regarding extending marriage; and on this they said "these are these" — implies their dispute too is regarding marriage; and per Perach it would seem plainly Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel do not dispute regarding extension — if so R' Meir and R' Yosi would not dispute the ancients' dispute; and the language "these are these" does not teach except that this dispute is relevant to that; and not that this is literally the dispute; and so is Rashi's explanation's implication who notes on Beit Hillel's words eighteen months and explains: sufficient for the infant with this; and if for the reason of permitting marriage at eighteen months — he should have noted eighteen months on R' Yosi's words; however not for the relevance of separation — if so it would be leniency of Beit Shammai; but only as Rashi explained for the infant's sufficiency; and Chelkat Mechokek on the father until how much he is obligated to strive in his nursing or feeding eggs so he not be endangered; but in Tosefta Niddah ch. 2 I found the version in this language after the dispute of R' Meir and R' Yosi: R' Natan ben Yosef said: Beit Shammai say twenty-four months and Beit Hillel say eighteen months — this implies per the Gaon Neriah's explanation; and the question is uncertain — unless therefore the Talmud changed the version and it is primary per Rashi's explanation.`,
});

patchFile("siman_013/beur-hagra/part-001.txt", "beur-hagra", {
  "1#_": `Every woman, etc. Like the first tanna; and so we say in the Gemara there — Ameimar permitted, etc.; and halakhah is, etc.`,
  "2#_": `Ninety days. So too there that Ameimar said, etc.; and so they said in the Yerushalmi there — Rav Assi said: ninety complete days; Shmuel said: yes, and their pregnancies.`,
  "3#_": `And from the day of writing, etc. There; and halakhah is, etc.; and the elders of Spain and Maggid Mishneh testified that so is the version in all old books; and so they said in the Yerushalmi ch. HaCholeitz: halakhah — some teach in the name of R' Chiya in the name of R' Meir: the chalutzah counts from the time of death; the divorcée from the time of giving her get; R' Chanina said from the time of giving her get; R' Yosi says from the time of writing; Rav and Shmuel — Rav like R' Chiya and Shmuel like R' Yosi, etc.; and halakhah: for a get we count from the time of writing if he did not seclude with her; and so Rav Ashi holds in the Gemara.`,
  "4#_": `And some say, etc. Like the books' version (and halakhah); and halakhah is like Rav regarding prohibition.`,
  "5#_": `And the decree, etc. As written — R' Yochanan retracted and explains like R' Meir and R' Yishmael; and so in ch. Af Al Pi — Shmuel like R' Meir; and so in ch. 4 Achim.`,
  "6#_": `And even if she was divorced, etc. In the mishnah there.`,
  "7#_": `And even if her husband, etc. Like imprisoned in bet din; and so forty in Gittin 18a: one who sends, etc.`,
  "8#_": `Or a saris. Like a barren woman and an ailonit.`,
  "9#_": `And even a virgin, etc. Mishnah; and as explained in the Gemara there 42b: what is this, etc.`,
  "10#_": `Yevamah, etc. For she is not less than an arusah; and she is closer to relations than an arusah; and specifically if she performed chalitzah she need not wait, for chalitzah is proven upon her like a me'unah — Tosafot there 1 s.v. achar, etc.`,
  "11#_": `She became pregnant, etc. As above there is no distinction; and like one who miscarries after death.`,
  "12#_": `He returns, etc. From what is written — Rava challenged: therefore, etc.; and if it were so — what is his challenge; behold in any case he holds a married convert generally must wait; and as written in Semag: and marriages in their gentilehood are nothing; and it is returning his divorcee; and so in Nedarim 91b: and I returned in my days; and the Ran wrote there: and I returned to her, etc.; but Ritva wrote he cited "I returned" because regarding another she is forbidden until ninety days; and mishnah there — R' Acha like R' Yehudah and R' Yosi in Yevamot.`,
  "13#_": `Who were married, etc. So Maggid Mishneh; and all that is written in these two seifim until "and the same applies to a captive" — is the Rambam's language; and Maggid Mishneh wrote he ruled in all like R' Yosi as the Rif wrote; only he questioned him from what is written: therefore convert and convertess — for there it deals with marriage; but here it deals with prostitution as written in the Gemara there; and the Rambam omitted maidservant; and so convert and convertess regarding prostitution — since we hold like R' Yosi even regarding an Israelite zona she need not wait — all the more regarding them; for Shmuel like R' Yehudah holds regarding a zona; and he holds like R' Yosi regarding convert and freed maidservant; and Nekudot Yosef: the strength of R' Yehudah comes to teach us regarding a convert.`,
  "14#_": `And the same applies to a captive. For this is to exclude the Raosh's view as written below.`,
});

patchFile("siman_013/turei-zahav/part-001.txt", "turei-zahav", {
  "1#א": `Until she waits ninety days — see two reasons in the Gemara.`,
  "1#ב": `That he may not cohabit, etc. Below he wrote that he must swear on this.`,
  "10#א": `And some are stringent — it is wondrous to me, for there Terumat HaDeshen likewise wrote siman 216 and he did not decide to forbid; and on the contrary he ruled there to permit per the first view that Or Zarua wrote: and so it seems that which we wait to divorce is specifically when he betrothed or married intentionally; but if he erred thinking she is not nursing or he does not know another's wet-nurse is forbidden — we do not coerce him to divorce, only separation without a get; and further it seems in the conclusion we do not fine for a get except regarding marriage; but betrothal suffices with mere separation; so he concludes; and he wrote on this that this is specifically such unintentional; but unintentional who erred in ruling the month of pregnancy is considered intentional; and in the conclusion he wrote that Or Zarua's writing that in pressing alone he need not give a get — we do not hold; for Tosafot ch. HaCholeitz in the name of She'iltot dispute on this; and possibly therefore they also are not lenient like the other matter of Or Zarua that if he married unintentionally to permit with mere separation; so he concludes — behold he only wrote as a possible way that one should not be lenient; and how do we abandon Or Zarua's plainness and rely on this possibility that has no proof at all; and further it is wondrous to me: per Terumat HaDeshen's conclusion there is no distinction between marriage and betrothal — why did Rama write this law specifically regarding betrothal; for in truth even regarding marriage permitted with a kohen's wife per Rama's decision who so divides to rule leniently with a kohen's wife — and this is Or Zarua's view — and this is even regarding marriage; and so seems primary to be lenient even regarding marriage with a kohen's wife; and even with an Israelite there is no reason to be stringent regarding unintentional — except Rama was stringent in this.`,
  "10#ב": `And it does not seem so — for we do not care what he can return within three months; rather since he revealed his intent he does not wish to marry her — it suffices; and one may say: since every person will do so — betroth in this city and flee to another city — one may say: since it became clear he fears bet din that they will rebuke him — he will certainly fear afterward to marry her.`,
  "11#א": `Another's pregnant woman. The reason in the Gemara regarding nursing: lest she become pregnant and her milk be lost and kill the child through hunger; plainly regarding another woman they say she will eat eggs and milk her husband gives her; not so here where the infant's father died and she will be ashamed to claim from his heirs; and the reason of another's pregnant woman they say in the Gemara: presumptively pregnant will nurse — meaning she will afterward nurse; and there is a question: if so why did the tanna teach wet-nurse; and one may say the reason teaches us: why is pregnant forbidden — because she will nurse; and if so the reverse question: why teach pregnant — certainly everyone knows she will eventually nurse; and one may say: there is a woman who was already nursing and established as nursing — she may not marry; but pregnant — perhaps they did not concern for nursing afterward, for perhaps she will not nurse him at all; it teaches us presumptively pregnant stands to nurse even though not yet established — as above.`,
  "11#ב": `And the month of pregnancy counts toward the number, etc. This is Hagahot Maimoniot ch. 1 of Hilchot Gerushin; he brings it in Terumat HaDeshen siman 216: his reason — since the Talmud cited twenty-four months for the infant and did not cite two years — implies in a leap year twenty-four months suffice; and the first answer (is) Maharam's view in Mordechai ch. HaCholeitz; and also in Hagahot Mordechai there: Terumat HaDeshen siman 216 — question: Reuven married another's wet-nurse after twenty-four months and counted the month of pregnancy as one of them; the matter became known to the sage and one of the great ones wished to separate him as the Talmud removes: ch. HaCholeitz — one who marries another's pregnant woman or wet-nurse removes with a get, etc.; Mordechai wrote in the name of Maharam: if the year is pregnant it is pregnant for the infant; and one of the great ones permitted post factum from the aforementioned Hagahot Maimoniot — not like Maharam's words; post factum he relied on the lenient one; and one of the great ones challenged Maharam's words against each other: here he was not precise since he cited twenty-four months and did not cite two years; and in his laws of rejoicing he was precise since he cited twelve months in mourning and did not cite a year — if the year was pregnant twelve months suffice; and so is the custom here ab initio like Maharam; and regarding the infant the custom is to forbid ab initio; and I found a responsum from that which we learned in ch. Mi sheAchazo: how long is his nursing — two years; R' Yehudah says twelve months; and Rashi explained there: R' Yehudah and the first tanna who is R' Meir dispute in that mishnah in that dispute they dispute in ch. Af Al Pi; R' Meir holds a wet-nurse is forbidden to marry until twenty-four months and R' Yosi holds after twelve months; and from here he inferred in that responsum since the beraita cited twenty-four months and in the mishnah in Mi sheAchazo two years — since one dispute — if so both languages stringently: that which cites two years comes to exclude the month of pregnancy that we do not count toward twenty-four months but two years from day to day; and he also cited twenty-four months because with years we could also explain per the world's year custom and complete it when Rosh Hashanah arrives as in Rosh Hashanah — one may say one day in a year after Rosh Hashanah counts as a year; therefore in the responsum he concludes post factum she need not be separated; and at first glance there is a good question on Hagahot Maimoniot who is lenient from what it cited two years — for we find explicitly in ch. Mi sheAchazo it cites truly two years; however as you were precise you will find Hagahot Maimoniot's words in my humble opinion are correct and Maharam's words are very wondrous for two reasons: first we see here there is more question why it cited in ch. Af Al Pi regarding prohibition to marry twenty-four months than the difficulty regarding mourning that we mentioned — for here we find two opposites in one matter: it cites here twenty-four months and regarding nursing measure in ch. Mi sheAchazo it cited two years; and what he answered in Terumat HaDeshen that both are stringent — behold his words contradict each other: first he said the language two years teaches to exclude the month of pregnancy that is not in the account; and afterward he said if it only cited two years and not twenty-four months — one would say even one day after Rosh Hashanah counts as a year; and if so one could say to you: where you say the language twenty-four months explains on thirty-two months that you should not say it teaches to be lenient on one day in a year — so too one could say it comes to explain that two years without pregnancy but twenty-five months we require with pregnancy — it teaches us the language twenty-four months that only twenty-four are needed; or the truth is as written at the end that the language two years teaches to be lenient even one day — for the beginning of presumption of houses we say explicitly three years from day to day — implies three years presumptively is not from day to day; and we do not find presumptive years that should specifically be from day to day except in ch. HaSho'el in renting a house for a year the leap month is included in the year — there it is not relevant to say his rental on one day in a year counts as a year; but in other places we certainly say to be lenient with presumptive year; and the correct way: certainly where years are mentioned there is no question at all why it cites years and did not cite months — for counting months is unique where they do not teach on the matter; but conversely there is a question: in ch. Af Al Pi it cited the language twenty-four months — sleeping from what is said in ch. Mi sheAchazo two years — behold certainly must answer it teaches us that the language two years has the explanation that even one day counts as a year — it teaches us that specifically we require twenty-four months; however twenty-four months suffice in any case and the month of pregnancy is included in the twenty-four months; and even if you wish to say presumptive two years in Mi sheAchazo means with the month of pregnancy — i.e., from day to day — behold we see the tanna in ch. Af Al Pi a year twenty-four months contradicts this — for we require only twenty-four months; how is it fit to say the tanna makes explanation on part of the language two years and say it does not suffice with one day and does not make explanation on part of the language two years and say it does not suffice with one day and does not make explanation on the entire language — i.e., implies with pregnancy; rather certainly the language twenty-four months in ch. Af Al Pi is specifically — from it one may not reduce and upon it one may not add; and the Talmud says with full mouth she must wait twenty-four months apart from the day it was born and apart from the day she was betrothed; and we do not further say apart from the month of pregnancy — rather certainly the month of pregnancy is like other months in this; second: for Maharam who wishes to be stringent and say the month of pregnancy is not included in twenty-four months — the stringency comes to leniency — for it is in the sugya ch. Af Al Pi: our rabbis taught: she nurses the infant and goes until twenty-four months — R' Eliezer; R' Yehoshua says even four and five years — meaning after twenty-four months he returns and nurses like nursing a reptile; and behold if you explain the month of pregnancy is added to twenty-four months for prohibition of marriage — you would also say thus regarding nursing measure: for R' Eliezer he nurses the leap year twenty-five months; and for R' Yehoshua if one explanation twenty-four months in the leap year he would have permission to return and nurse; and the tanna says explicitly twenty-four months there is permission and no more; and if his intent were to permit in a leap year — he should have said for R' Eliezer permission of nursing until two years; for R' Yosi if another explanation (two years); and why did they mention the months that are not precise per Maharam; rather certainly plain that after twenty-four months even in a leap year nursing days are completed; and forbidden for R' Eliezer to nurse more; and for R' Yosi if explanation forbidden to return; and if so regarding prohibition of marriage certainly twenty-four months is the precise language; and there is no distinction between leap month and another month at all; for we did not read two women as should have been read from the language side as cited in Mi sheAchazo — rather to teach that this language is precise without addition or subtraction ever; and behold even though we have no power to divide, heaven forbid, on Maharam — nevertheless I wrote my view because it is Torah and I need to learn Torah; and added to this: since there is dispute before us between the rishonim — i.e., Hagahot Maimoniot is lenient not like Maharam as Terumat HaDeshen wrote — Rama's language implies he does not decide to be stringent but on the contrary first rules plainly that the month of pregnancy is from the count; only some say to be stringent ab initio; and in a rabbinic matter we certainly follow leniency where there is dispute; and behold an incident came before me: a woman whose child was twenty-four months on Rosh Chodesh Adar Sheni; and I said that in this even the author of Terumat HaDeshen agrees she is permitted to marry — for there he was stringent only regarding a woman who counted twenty-four months with the month of pregnancy — there is somewhat room to be stringent not to bring this month into the account; but here twenty-four months passed before pregnancy — only you come to add another one month — this is not reasonable even for a Torah scholar; and further in my humble opinion: in every case if there is some side to be lenient otherwise — even though it is not sufficient to be lenient — nevertheless it combines for her that the month of pregnancy is also included as above to lean lenient since here there is only a rabbinic matter and we follow leniency in place of dispute; and all the more since we clarified the view that rules to be lenient is Hagahot Maimoniot — it is reasonable per the halakhah sugya.`,
  "11#ג": `And he hired a wet-nurse for him. In the Tur the version is "or he hired"; and even with this version it would be "and he hired" like "or he hired" — for if per Beit Yosef's explanation "and he hired" refers to what precedes — we need two matters; why afterward "in her husband's lifetime" since they already wrote so — Maharach; and accordingly if he hired a wet-nurse alone it suffices even near her husband's death; and if so there is a question: what is written afterward — a different division: or she gave to a wet-nurse three months, etc.; and in the Tur truly this is not the division at all; rather there is a question: behold Beit Yosef wrote the Rosh in responsum was astonished at what he saw in the Geonim's words this division of three months before her husband's death; and on this the Rosh wrote: it seems they knew that within three months milk can return to the woman and not afterward; and so is his conclusion for practical law; also Rivash wrote in the name of responsum of the Ran this law of three months — therefore it seems primary as Beit Yosef wrote regarding the law of milk stopping for the child: not sufficient except specifically after three months; and that the Tur did not mention three months — because it is not a fixed matter; for Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Rivash that what the Gaon wrote three months — he holds the majority's milk stops after three months; but there are those who stop after one or two months; and if so we explain the Tur's words: what is written "or her milk stopped" — meaning the known measure for her milk stopping; or "she hired a wet-nurse for him in her husband's lifetime" — meaning even though her milk did not stop but of good will she hired him a wet-nurse in her husband's lifetime as the poskim's measure for milk stopping; and per the version "and she hired" it also seems not sufficient if she hired less than three months or measure of milk stopping — only a question what is missing and she is not his wet-nurse entirely even a small amount but she hired him a wet-nurse — and from this the child's life; and this is in any case as the measure of her milk stopping; and one may not challenge this version "and she hired" — for the Tur cited in explanation in bet haba it seems his intent: if he did not write "in her husband's lifetime" only afterward "and she hired" — this means after her husband's death her milk stopped and before her husband's death she hired him a wet-nurse even a short time — it teaches us we require it stopped in her husband's lifetime — i.e., the poskim's measure; and in any case three months suffice for all women.`,
  "14#_": `Before she was divorced, etc. This is the Rashba's view that Beit Yosef brings — there is a distinction between divorcee and widow; and this view Tosafot, Rosh, and Mordechai wrote in the name of Rabbeinu Shimshon: Tosafot ch. Af Al Pi 60: Rabbeinu Shimshon says a divorcee is permitted to marry for she is not obligated to nurse as taught above; and Rabbenu Tam says forbidden to marry for it is taught in ch. HaCholeitz another's pregnant woman and wet-nurse he may not marry; and he decides and the tanna taught "presumptively widow" — "presumptively divorcee"; so he concludes; and they also brought another proof for this; and so the Rosh ch. HaCholeitz in the name of She'iltot: "presumptively divorcee" — "presumptively widow"; and if so the Rashba must hold like Rabbeinu Shimshon; and if so Shulchan Aruch should not have brought this view in this seif since he ruled above seif 11 whether widow whether divorcee; and since in the Gemara's language that said he may not marry another's wet-nurse — widow and divorcee are included — certainly there is no distinction between them in any matter; and Maharach distinguishes between divorcee and widow regarding if the infant does not recognize her and she says she does not wish to nurse — regarding divorcee she may marry; and I do not recognize this distinction at all — for what do we care about her statement; certainly every divorcee would say so and nullify the Sages' enactment that they enacted also regarding a divorcee; and Beit Yosef wrote: and I saw authorities like the Rashba and I did not suppress them since they have whom to rely on; so he concludes; and certainly one should not rule leniency in this as Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Rivash; only if she married she need not give a get in this — in this one may rely on the Rashba.`,
});
